# "A Common Pride and Fame": The Attack and Repulse of Pickett's Division, July 3, 1863, Part I: second reading (the Angle wall, the road fence and the Hummelbaugh fences; filed after 85-common-pride-1.xdef so that no earlier extract id moves).
# Typescript with light OCR noise: quotes are typed in the cleaned form with the explicit {{saved|fixed}} marks where a letter of the text layer is wrong; the five pages quoted were looked at as page images.
# The study quotes soldiers' accounts: the claim says whose account it is, and an account is as good as its source.
@ NPS-COMMON-PRIDE-1
%images 92, 135, 143, 158, 189

%basis Captain Andrew Cowan, 1st New York Independent Battery, an account quoted by the study (the page introduces it as 'another account by Cowan')
143/128 | fences | The wall at the Angle was very low, not over two feet; three or four rails stake and rider fashion had made it high enough to turn cattle; too little soil to raise it | P-01,E-17,FEN-cemetery-ridge-walls | 1863-07-03 | stated | The study quotes Captain Cowan's account that the infantry at the wall had very slight protection because the wall was very low in the angle, certainly not over two feet high, that three or four rails stake and rider fashion had previously made it high enough to turn cattle, and that there was not enough soil there to strengthen the wall and raise it higher | =Our infantry at the wall had {{veryyslight|very slight}} protection, because it was very low in the angle, certainly not over two feet high. Three or four rails, stake and rider fashion, had previously made it high enough to turn cattle. There was not enough soil there to strengthen the wall and raise it higher.
%basis an account quoted in the study's discussion of Cowan's battery (the page shows no author; the account is of the battery commander's position at the Angle)
189/173 | fences | The low stone wall at the Angle had been thrown down in places on the second day to let the guns through; rails used at the gaps | P-01,E-17,FEN-cemetery-ridge-walls | 1863-07-03 | stated | The study quotes an account that this low stone wall had been thrown down in places to let the guns through on the second day, that rails had been used for additional shelter at these gaps and in other places, and that it was the only breastwork or fortification protecting Webb's infantry on the left of the trees | #s11-12
%basis -
158/142 | fences | The east-west fence from the Emmitsburg Road to the Angle wall at the slashing, the northern boundary of the Codori farm | FEN-emmitsburg-road,bld-codori-farm | 1863-07-03 | hedged | The study says a fence runs on a general east-west line from the Emmitsburg Road to the Angle wall at the slashing and defined the northern boundary of the Codori Farm, that it would have been the only fence between the road and the Union defenses that Garnett's Brigade would have crossed, and that it is still part of the battlefield landscape (whether the present fence is original is not said) | #s7-8
%basis J. W. Nesbit, 149th Pennsylvania, "Recollections of Pickett's Charge", National Tribune, 16 November 1916 (the study's footnote 106)
92/77 | fences | No stone walls on the Warren or Cope maps in the area of Stone's brigade except worm or stone-and-rider fencing around the Hummelbaugh fields; the nearest such fence 400-500 ft from the Union first line | bld-hummelbaugh-farm | ~1868-1890s (Warren and Cope maps as read by the study; the soldier's 'broken-down stone fence' it comments on is of 1863) | stated | The study's note says no stone walls are indicated on the Warren or Cope maps in that area except for worm or stone and rider fencing enclosing the Hummelbaugh farm fields, and that the Warren map puts the closest of these fences 400-500 feet from the Union first line and some distance behind (east of) the crest of Cemetery Ridge, so the soldier's broken-down stone fence cannot be placed | =No stone walls are indicated on the Warren or Cope maps in that area except for worm or stone and rider fencing enclosing the Hummelbaugh farm fields. However, the Warren map indicates that the closest of these fences would have been 400-500 feet from the Union first line, and some distance behind (or east) of the crest of Cemetery Ridge.
%basis S. A. Ashe, "The Pickett-Pettigrew Charge" (the study's footnote 169), quoted as a description of the Union position
135/120 | fields | Skirmishers lay in the clover and grass in front of the Union line; a strong fence along the pike in front of Hays; a low stone wall further down the valley | FEN-emmitsburg-road | 1863-07-03 | stated | The study quotes a description of the Union position that skirmishers lay out several hundred yards in front in the clover and grass, that a first line of infantry held a strong fence along the pike in front of Hays and a low stone wall further down the valley, and lay concealed in the grass in the intervening space | =Skirmishers lay out several hundred yards in front in the clover and grass, while a first line of infantry held a strong fence along the pike in front of Hays and a low stone wall further down the valley, and lay concealed in the grass in the intervening space.
%basis G. W. Finley, Company K, 56th Virginia, "Bloody Angle", an account quoted by the study (footnote 194)
155/139 | fences | The board fences along the Emmitsburg Road were still mostly standing when the 56th Virginia reached the road (a soldier's account) | FEN-emmitsburg-road | 1863-07-03 | conflict | The study quotes a Virginian's account that when his regiment struck the road the board fences were still mostly standing and caused a momentary check until the men went against and over them; another passage of the study says the road fences near Codori's house were nearly destroyed the day before, so the condition of the road fences during the charge is carried as a conflict | =When my regiment struck the road the board fences were still mostly standing and there was a momentary check until our men went against and over them. | @131: =The fences of the Emmitsburg road, near Cordovie’s house, had been nearly destroyed the day before in the battle on our right.
